    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "./mystery > /tmp/out.txt 2>&1; echo EXIT=$?\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat -A /tmp/out.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 64 image.ppm | od -An -tx1 | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "stat -c '%s' image.ppm\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Tools available: strings, objdump, gcc, readelf, nm, python3, head, od. The previous commands were sent but output not yet shown. Let me wait for the results.",
  "plan": "Wait for the output of the run, cat -A, head, and stat commands.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key findings: The program prints a fixed intro, then prints progress with carriage returns (`\\rProgress: X.X%`) stepping through values following a repeating difference pattern [+0.2,+0.1,+0.2] — which matches percent = i*100/600 for i=0..599 (600 rows). After the loop it prints '\\nRendering complete\\nWrote image to image.ppm\\nDone.'. It writes a P3 ASCII PPM, 800x600, max 255, with integer RGB pixels, file size 5,362,624 bytes. I have gcc, objdump, strings, python3. I can iterate: compare my recompiled program's output to the original byte-for-byte. First I'll save a reference copy of the image and stdout, count the prints, and analyze the pixel data to deduce the formula.",
  "plan": "1. Regenerate reference: run mystery, save stdout to /tmp/ref_out.txt and image to /tmp/ref.ppm. 2. Count 'Progress:' occurrences (expect 600). 3. Write a python script to parse the PPM and analyze its structure (RGB ranges, whether it's a function of x,y, math functions). 4. Examine strings/disassembly for confirmation of format strings and rendering math. Then write mystery.c and iterate until byte-identical.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "./mystery > /tmp/ref_out.txt 2>&1; cp image.ppm /tmp/ref.ppm; echo done\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -c Progress /tmp/ref_out.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "tail -c 80 /tmp/ref.ppm | od -An -c | tail -5\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Confirmed: progress is all on one logical line using carriage returns (grep -c returns 1). The PPM is P3 ASCII, 800x600, max 255. Tail shows pixel values like '67 67 67' and '152 152 152' — small integers, possibly R==G==B (grayscale) or computed. I need to determine the exact pixel formula. Let me examine the binary's strings and analyze the PPM numerically with python.",
  "plan": "1. Run strings on the binary to find format strings and any embedded math/constants. 2. Write a python script to parse the P3 PPM into a pixel array and analyze: check min/max, whether R==G==B, deduce the formula as function of (x,y). This will reveal the rendering math.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "strings -n 5 mystery | grep -vi 'GLIBC\\|gcc\\|GCC\\|GNU' | head -60\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

